   "text": {
    "L1": "Area tells us how much surface a shape covers.",
    "L2": "We can find it by counting the unit squares inside the shape.",
    "L3": "A 9 centimetre by 4 centimetre rectangle has 9 squares in each row and 4 rows.",
    "L4": "So its area is length times breadth: 9 times 4 equals 36 square centimetres.",
    "L5": "The unit is square centimetres, not centimetres, because we counted squares.",
    "L6": "Here is a myth: equal perimeters must give equal areas. Not true: a 6 by 2 rectangle and a 4 by 4 square both have perimeter 16, but their areas are 12 and 16 square units.",
    "L7": "For a fixed perimeter, the rectangle closer to a square has the larger area.",
    "L8": "Now choose the area of the 9 centimetre by 4 centimetre rectangle."
   },
